From cccc262bbaed003ef1572723ee21e6ac5cd8ff6b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PePe Amengual <2208324+jamengual@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 10:44:46 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] nullfs: refuse to open device nodes

A nullfs mount of devfs (mount -t nullfs /dev /x, or a container
runtime bind-mounting a host /dev into a jail) exposes every device of
the host devfs, whatever the jail's devfs ruleset hides.  read(2) and
write(2) on such a device fail with ENXIO, because the file gets vnops
and devfs's dead_read/dead_write run, so the view looks harmless.  But
open(2) runs the driver's d_open and ioctl(2) reaches d_ioctl through
null_bypass() and devfs_ioctl(): a jailed root can, for example, issue
DIOCGDELETE on a host disk.

Fail the open of a VCHR vnode with ENXIO, the error read(2) already
returns.  Device nodes outside devfs have no driver, so only devfs
devices are affected, and those can't be read or written through
nullfs today.
---
 sys/fs/nullfs/null_vnops.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/sys/fs/nullfs/null_vnops.c b/sys/fs/nullfs/null_vnops.c
index ee7d89db6..9b8c95dd6 100644
--- a/sys/fs/nullfs/null_vnops.c
+++ b/sys/fs/nullfs/null_vnops.c
@@ -512,6 +512,15 @@ null_open(struct vop_open_args *ap)
 	struct vnode *vp, *ldvp;
 
 	vp = ap->a_vp;
+	/*
+	 * Devices are reached only through devfs, whose ruleset decides
+	 * which ones a jail sees.  Through nullfs, read(2) and write(2)
+	 * on a device already fail (the file gets vnops, not devfs's
+	 * fileops), but the driver's open and ioctl routines would
+	 * still run, past that ruleset.  Refuse the open.
+	 */
+	if (vp->v_type == VCHR)
+		return (ENXIO);
 	ldvp = NULLVPTOLOWERVP(vp);
 	retval = null_bypass(&ap->a_gen);
 	if (retval == 0) {
